# SKILL.md — repo_health_audit (v1.0)

## Metadata
```yaml
playbook_id: repo_health_audit
version: 1.0.0
classification: CodemindIDE / Discovery-Agent
taxonomy: ACS Component Taxonomy (auto-resolved via identify_component_type)
terminology: CCC (Component Criticality Classification) / TABR (Triage-Assign-Baseline-Remediate)
determinism: strict — identical repo state at commit SHA X must always yield identical findings hash
idempotent: true
mongo_writes: mandatory
execution_modes: [single_repo, batch_50k via codemind_batch.py]
estimated_runtime: 45s-4min per repo depending on size/history depth
```

## Purpose
Single end-to-end playbook that produces a complete repository health record in one pass —
deep security analysis, dependency risk, code health, config hygiene, CI/CD maturity,
ownership risk, and license compliance — instead of orchestrating 6-8 separate playbooks per
repo. Designed for the 50k-repo batch sweep: one graphifyy index build, one git history walk,
one output schema. Language-agnostic by design — every stage runs against a normalized
intermediate representation, not language-specific source parsing, so the same pipeline covers
Java/Spring repos alongside Python, JS/TS, Go, C#, Ruby, and anything else in the estate
without a separate playbook per language.

## Why one playbook instead of many
- graphifyy AST/code-graph indexing is the single most expensive step (OOM risk already seen
  on uaap-codemind at 1000+ concurrent repos). Building it once and reusing the in-memory graph
  across all sub-scans avoids re-indexing 8x per repo across 50k repos.
- One deterministic output schema means AgentGuard, Litmus, and the Discovery Agent UI consume
  one document shape instead of stitching together 8 playbook outputs per repo.
- One MongoDB write transaction per repo instead of 8 reduces write amplification at 50k scale.

## Pipeline stages (sequential, each stage gated on the prior)

### Stage 0 — Preflight
1. Resolve component identity via `identify_component_type` (ACS Component Taxonomy) — this
   determines which stage-specific rule packs load (e.g. Spring Boot transaction rules only
   load if component_type includes `spring-boot`).
2. Resolve CCC tier for the repo (criticality classification) — determines remediation SLA
   bucketing applied to every finding downstream, per TABR.
3. Check MongoDB for a prior run at the same commit SHA. If found and hash-identical, short
   circuit and return the cached record (determinism guarantee, avoids redundant compute).
4. Acquire graphifyy index. Build if absent; reuse if present. Enforce the per-pod concurrency
   cap fix (max N simultaneous graphifyy builds per node) to avoid the prior OOMKill class of
   failure.
5. **Language detection & analyzer resolution**: scan the repo tree (extensions, manifest
   files, shebangs) to produce a `language_profile` — e.g. `{java: 62%, python: 30%, sql: 8%}`
   for a polyglot repo. For every language present, resolve the matching analyzer adapter from
   the table below. A repo with N languages runs N adapters feeding into the same normalized
   graph — this is what keeps Stages 1-8 language-agnostic rather than requiring a rewrite per
   language.

   | Language | Parser / graphifyy frontend | Dependency manifest | Security rule pack |
   |---|---|---|---|
   | Java / Kotlin | JavaParser / tree-sitter-java | `pom.xml`, `build.gradle` | CCC-Java (Spring, JDBC, deserialization) |
   | Python | tree-sitter-python / ast module | `requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml`, `Pipfile` | CCC-Python (pickle, eval/exec, subprocess, Django/Flask) |
   | JavaScript / TypeScript | tree-sitter-typescript | `package.json` + lockfiles | CCC-JS (prototype pollution, DOM XSS, Node child_process) |
   | Go | tree-sitter-go / go/ast | `go.mod` | CCC-Go (unchecked errors, goroutine leaks, exec.Command) |
   | C# / .NET | Roslyn / tree-sitter-c-sharp | `*.csproj`, `packages.config` | CCC-DotNet (deserialization, LINQ injection) |
   | Ruby | tree-sitter-ruby | `Gemfile` | CCC-Ruby (Rails mass assignment, YAML.load) |
   | C / C++ | tree-sitter-cpp | `Makefile`/`CMakeLists.txt` | CCC-Native (buffer overflow, memory safety, unsafe funcs) |
   | SQL / PL-SQL | tree-sitter-sql | n/a | CCC-SQL (dynamic SQL construction, missing parameterization) |
   | Shell / Bash | tree-sitter-bash | n/a | CCC-Shell (injection via unquoted vars, eval) |
   | Terraform / YAML / Helm | HCL / YAML parsers | n/a | CCC-IaC (see Stage 5) |

   A language with no adapter yet falls back to a **generic regex/entropy pass** (secrets,
   obvious hardcoded credentials, TODO/FIXME security markers) rather than being silently
   skipped — every file in the repo is touched by at least one pass, even without full AST
   support.
6. Normalize every language's AST into graphifyy's common intermediate graph representation
   (nodes: functions/methods/classes; edges: calls, imports, data-flow) so Stages 3, 4, and 8
   operate on one graph shape regardless of source language.

### Stage 1 — Git history extraction (single walk, feeds Stages 3, 6, 7)
Walk full commit history once; extract and cache in-memory:
- Per-file: commit count, unique author count, last-modified date, revert count, lines
  churned (added+removed) over trailing 90/180/365 day windows
- Per-author: files touched, commit frequency, last-active date (feeds bus-factor calc)
- Commit message corpus: conventional-commit compliance rate, revert-commit ratio
- Tag/release history: release cadence, days-since-last-tag, hotfix-tag frequency (tags
  within 48h of a prior tag, signal of unstable releases)
- Branch metadata via GitHub API: open PR count, average PR age, average review-comment
  count per PR, stale-branch count (no commits in 90+ days)

### Stage 2 — Dependency & license scan
1. Parse manifest files present in repo: `pom.xml`, `package.json`, `requirements.txt`,
   `go.mod`, `Gemfile`, lockfiles for all of the above.
2. For each resolved dependency (direct + transitive, using graphifyy's lockfile-parsing
   enhancements for transitive resolution):
   - Query OSV + GitHub Advisory DB for known CVEs → severity + CVSS
   - Resolve latest available version + EOL/deprecation date from registry metadata
   - Resolve declared license → cross-check against an allow/deny list (copyleft flagged)
3. Compute blast radius per vulnerable/EOL dependency: count of internal modules importing
   it, via graphifyy graph traversal — this is what separates "CVE in an unused dev-only
   package" from "CVE in something 40 services depend on."
4. Emit one row per dependency with: version, latest, days-to-EOL, CVE list, license,
   blast_radius_score.

### Stage 3 — Code health scan (graphifyy-powered)
1. **Dead/orphan code**: nodes in the AST graph with zero inbound edges repo-wide, ranked by
   last-modified date from Stage 1 (old + orphaned = high-confidence dead code; recently
   added + orphaned = likely in-progress, lower confidence).
2. **Resilience pattern detection**: reuse existing resilience-pattern-detection logic —
   circuit breakers, retries, timeouts present/absent on outbound calls identified in the
   graph.
3. **Transaction boundary audit** (Spring components only, gated by Stage 0 component type):
   flag `@Transactional` self-invocation, propagation misconfiguration, missing rollback
   rules — detected via call-graph analysis of proxy-bypassing internal calls.
4. **Test classification**: reuse existing test-classification logic; cross-reference against
   Stage 1's revert/churn data to flag files with high churn but low/no test coverage as
   priority gaps, and files with frequent CI reruns (if CI history available) as flaky
   candidates rather than genuinely broken.

### Stage 4 — Deep Security Analysis (core section — largest stage by design)
This is the anchor stage of the playbook, not a thin wrapper. It runs multiple independent
sub-scans against the normalized graph from Stage 0.6, each producing findings in the same
CCC/TABR schema, then correlates across sub-scans before emitting. It reuses
`ccc_security_vulnerability_scan` (v4.0) and `ise_detect_sensitive_data_exposure` as two of the
sub-routines, not as the whole stage.

**4.1 — SAST / taint analysis (deep, cross-function)**
- Build source→sink taint paths across the whole call graph (not per-file): every user-facing
  input source (HTTP params, headers, cookies, file uploads, message-queue payloads, gRPC
  fields) is traced through the graph to every dangerous sink (SQL execution, shell exec,
  file path construction, deserialization, template rendering, LDAP/XML query construction,
  reflection/dynamic class loading).
- Path is only reported as exploitable if it crosses the boundary with **no matching sanitizer
  node** on the path (parameterized query, allow-list validator, encoder) — this is what
  makes it "deep" rather than single-line pattern matching: a `String.format` building SQL is
  only a finding if it actually reaches `Statement.execute`, not merely present in a file that
  also happens to touch a DB somewhere else.
- Injection classes covered per language rule pack from Stage 0.5: SQLi, NoSQLi (Mongo
  operator injection), command injection, LDAP injection, XPath/XXE, SSTI (template engines),
  header/CRLF injection, prototype pollution (JS), unsafe deserialization (Java
  `ObjectInputStream`, Python `pickle`, .NET `BinaryFormatter`, Ruby `Marshal.load`/`YAML.load`).

**4.2 — Authentication & authorization audit**
- Locate every route/endpoint/handler node in the graph and check for a reachable
  authn/authz gate (annotation, middleware, decorator per framework) between the route and
  any sensitive operation it performs.
- Flag: missing auth on state-changing endpoints, authz checks performed client-side only,
  IDOR patterns (object ID taken directly from request and used in a DB lookup with no
  ownership check on the path), privilege-escalation-shaped code (role checks that can be
  bypassed via an alternate code path reaching the same sink).
- JWT/session handling: flag missing signature verification, `alg: none` acceptance, hardcoded
  signing keys, missing expiry checks — matched against the CVE/CWE class, not just keyword
  search.

**4.3 — Cryptography misuse**
- Flag weak/broken primitives (MD5/SHA1 for anything security-relevant, DES/RC4, ECB mode),
  hardcoded keys/IVs/salts, insufficient key length, predictable random (`Math.random`/
  `java.util.Random` used for tokens/session IDs instead of a CSPRNG), and missing certificate
  validation (disabled TLS verification, custom trust managers that accept all certs).

**4.4 — SSRF & outbound request analysis**
- Trace every outbound HTTP/network call node back to see if any part of the destination
  (host, port, path) is attacker-influenced without an allow-list check on the path — the
  same taint-path methodology as 4.1, applied to egress instead of injection sinks.

**4.5 — Secrets & sensitive data exposure (deep pass)**
- Runs `ise_detect_sensitive_data_exposure` plus an entropy-based scan across all file types
  (not just config, per Stage 5) and git history (not just HEAD) — a secret committed and
  later removed is still a live credential and still a finding unless there's evidence of
  rotation (paired with a later commit touching the same key name).
- Classifies by data sensitivity tier (credentials, PII, PCI-scoped fields, internal-only)
  using the same taxonomy as `ise_detect_sensitive_data_exposure` so findings merge cleanly.

**4.6 — Supply chain & build integrity**
- Cross-reference Stage 2's dependency list against typosquat-pattern package names, packages
  published to the registry in the last N days (new/low-reputation package risk), and
  dependencies pulled from non-standard/unpinned registries or via `git+https` refs without a
  commit-pinned SHA.
- Flag build scripts (`postinstall` hooks, Maven/Gradle plugins, Makefile targets) that fetch
  and execute remote content at build time.
- Flag CI workflow files (cross-referenced with Stage 6) that use unpinned third-party Actions
  (`uses: someaction@main` instead of a pinned SHA) — a known supply-chain vector.

**4.7 — IaC & container security (in-repo only)**
- For Terraform/Helm/K8s manifests found in-repo (full infra-registry scanning stays out of
  scope per Explicit non-goals): flag overly permissive IAM/RBAC (`*` resource/action),
  public-facing storage/network resources with no justification annotation, missing
  resource limits, containers running as root/privileged, base images pinned to `latest`.

**4.8 — Correlation & exploitability scoring**
Before emission, cross-reference every finding from 4.1-4.7 against:
- Stage 2's blast radius (a taint path ending in a vulnerable dependency is scored higher
  than either finding alone)
- Stage 1's churn/ownership data (a finding in a file with no active owner is flagged for
  escalated review, since normal PR-review pressure won't catch it)
- CCC tier from Stage 0.2 to set the TABR SLA bucket (a CCC-1 SSRF finding is `immediate`;
  the same finding in a CCC-4 internal tool may bucket to `next_sprint`)

Every finding across 4.1-4.7 carries: `cwe_id`, `owasp_category`, `taint_path` (source → sink,
list of graph node IDs, when applicable), `exploitability_score`, `confidence` (only
`confirmed` findings with a full traced path or an unambiguous pattern match are emitted —
speculative/heuristic-only signals are suppressed rather than reported as findings, keeping
the determinism guarantee from Stage 4 through Stage 9).

### Stage 5 — Config & secrets hygiene
1. Locate all `application.yml`/`.properties`, Helm `values*.yaml`, and Kubernetes manifests.
2. Diff config values across environment variants present in-repo (dev/stage/prod) to surface
   drift (a value present in prod but missing/different in staging, etc.).
3. Flag hardcoded secrets/credentials not externalized (pattern + entropy based, same
   determinism bar as Stage 4 — no fuzzy/LLM-judgment calls on what counts as a secret).
4. Flag non-backward-compatible DB migration changes: parse Flyway/Liquibase changesets for
   dropped columns, non-nullable columns added without defaults, against the schema baseline
   stored from the previous run (if present in Mongo) or against migration order if no
   baseline exists yet.

### Stage 6 — CI/CD & build maturity
1. Parse `.github/workflows/*.yml` and/or `Jenkinsfile` to inventory: build steps, test steps,
   presence of security-scan steps, deployment gates, required-approval rules.
2. If CI history is queryable (GitHub Actions API / Jenkins API), compute build/test duration
   trend over trailing 90 days and correlate spikes against dependency/plugin version bumps
   from Stage 2's data.
3. Score CI pipeline maturity 0-100 against a fixed rubric (test step present, security scan
   present, deploy approval gate present, build time within SLA, etc.) — rubric is fixed and
   versioned so scores are comparable across runs and repos.

### Stage 7 — Ownership & bus-factor report
Using Stage 1's per-author data: compute bus factor per module (min authors accounting for
80% of commits in trailing 12 months), flag single-owner modules, cross-reference against
CCC tier (a CCC-1 single-owner module is a materially different risk than a CCC-4 one).

### Stage 8 — API contract drift (if OpenAPI/Swagger spec present)
Diff the current spec against the spec at the previous recorded commit SHA (from Mongo).
Classify changes as breaking (removed field, tightened validation, changed type) vs.
additive, feeding directly into AgentGuard's PR impact scoring for any repo exposing an API.

### Stage 9 — Aggregation & MongoDB write
1. Merge all stage outputs into one `repo_health_record` document (schema below).
2. Compute a single composite `findings_hash` (stable sort + hash of all findings) —
   this is the determinism check used by Stage 0's short-circuit on future runs.
3. Bucket every finding into a TABR remediation SLA per its CCC tier and severity.
4. Write one transaction to MongoDB. Never partial-write; if any stage failed, mark the
   whole record `status: partial` with a `failed_stages` list rather than silently omitting
   data — downstream consumers must be able to distinguish "clean" from "incomplete."

