## Determinism requirements
- No stage may use an LLM to make a pass/fail judgment call on a finding — LLM use, if any,
  is restricted to natural-language summarization of already-computed findings, never to
  decide whether something IS a finding.
- All thresholds (churn windows, bus-factor %, CI maturity rubric weights) are versioned
  constants in this file, not runtime-configurable per invocation, so two runs at the same
  commit SHA always produce the same findings_hash.
- Stage short-circuiting (Stage 0.3) is the only caching allowed; every other stage runs
  fresh — no partial-result caching within a run.

## Batch execution (codemind_batch.py)
- Concurrency cap: reuse the existing per-node graphifyy build cap from the uaap-codemind
  OOMKill fix; this playbook's Stage 0.4 is the same bottleneck at greater severity since it's
  now the only graphifyy build per repo across the whole sweep (net win vs. 8 separate
  playbooks each building their own index).
- Checkpointing: write Stage 9's document per-repo immediately (not batched at sweep end) so
  a killed batch job resumes from the last completed repo via the Stage 0.3 short-circuit.
- Failure isolation: a Stage 3-8 failure on one repo must not abort the batch; record
  `status: partial` and continue.

## Explicit non-goals
- Not a replacement for SpotBugs/PMD/SonarQube — Stage 4.1 can ingest their output as an
  additional signal where already configured in-repo, but the taint-analysis core does not
  depend on them and runs standalone.
- Not a full container-registry/cloud-posture scanner — Stage 4.7 covers IaC/container config
  found *in the repo itself*; scanning live cloud accounts, running container images, or an
  image registry is a separate `infra_health_audit` playbook sharing this one's Mongo schema
  conventions but running against a different artifact set and schedule.
- Not a CI/CD executor — Stage 6 reads pipeline definitions and history, it does not trigger
  builds or gate merges; that enforcement lives in AgentGuard.
- Not a dynamic scanner (DAST/IAST/fuzzing) — every finding in Stage 4 is static/source-derived
  by design, to preserve the determinism guarantee (a DAST finding depends on runtime
  environment state, which breaks the "same commit SHA → same findings_hash" contract). Runtime
  verification of Stage 4 findings, if wanted, is a downstream consumer's job (e.g. feeding
  confirmed taint paths into a targeted fuzzing/pen-test queue), not this playbook's.
- A language with no adapter in the Stage 0.5 table is not silently excluded, but it also
  doesn't get full taint-path analysis — it gets the generic regex/entropy fallback only, and
  its `language_profile` entry is flagged `adapter: generic` so consumers know the coverage
  level differs from a fully-adapted language.
